<BaseLayout
  title="IRS primer"
  description="A short primer on the US tax rules for donated clothing and household items: fair market value, good used condition, substantiation tiers, Form 8283 and the 2026 changes."
>
  <h1>IRS primer</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The rules that govern a deduction for donated clothing and household items, in plain language, with the
    sources. Read the IRS publications themselves before relying on any of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Fair market value</h2>
  <p>
    The deduction for donated property is based on its fair market value (FMV). Treas. Reg. section 1.170A-1(c)(2)
    defines FMV as the price at which the property would change hands between a willing buyer and a willing seller,
    neither being under any compulsion to buy or sell and both having reasonable knowledge of the relevant facts.
  </p>
  <p>
    IRS Publication 561, <em>Determining the Value of Donated Property</em>, applies that test to used clothing and
    household items. It says the price that buyers of used items actually pay in used clothing stores, such as
    consignment or thrift shops, is an indication of the value, and that the valuation of used clothing "does not
    lend itself to fixed formulas or methods." Publication 561 also ranks the evidence: the item's own sale price,
    then comparable sales in the same market, then price guides, which are treated as clues that should be
    supported by other evidence. If you use a price guide, use the edition current at the date of the contribution.
  </p>
  <p>
    Publication 526, <em>Charitable Contributions</em>, adds that formulas such as a percentage of the cost of a new
    replacement item are not acceptable for valuing household items.
  </p>

  <h2>"Good used condition or better"</h2>
  <p>
    No deduction is allowed for clothing or a household item unless it is in good used condition or better (Internal
    Revenue Code section 170(f)(16), described in Publication 526). The one exception is a single item for which you
    claim more than $500: that item may be deducted regardless of condition only if you obtain a qualified appraisal
    and attach Form 8283 with Section B completed. "Household items" include furniture, furnishings, electronics,
    appliances and linens, but not food, art, antiques, jewelry, gems or collections.
  </p>

  <h2>Substantiation tiers</h2>
  <p>The record-keeping requirements are cumulative. Each tier adds to the ones below it.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Amount</th><th>What you need</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Any amount under $250</td>
        <td>A receipt from the organization showing its name, the date and location, and a description of the
        property, plus your own written records of the item's condition, how you acquired it and how you figured its
        FMV. A receipt is not required only where getting one is impractical, such as an unattended drop site.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>$250 or more per contribution</td>
        <td>A contemporaneous written acknowledgment from the organization describing the property and stating
        whether you received any goods or services in return.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>More than $500 in total noncash gifts for the year</td>
        <td>Form 8283, Section A, filed with your return, including how and when you acquired each item and its cost
        basis.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>More than $5,000 for one item or a group of similar items</td>
        <td>A qualified appraisal by a qualified appraiser and Form 8283, Section B, signed by the appraiser and the
        organization.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>Similar items are aggregated.</strong> The $5,000 threshold applies to the total of all similar items
    (for example, all clothing, or all furniture) you donate during the year, added up across every organization you
    gave to. Many separate trips do not avoid it. The Tax Court has disallowed entire clothing deductions where the
    aggregate exceeded $5,000 and no appraisal was obtained.
  </p>

  <h2>Form 8283 and "Thrift shop value"</h2>
  <p>
    Form 8283, Section A, asks in column (i) for the method used to determine FMV. The instructions list "Thrift
    shop value" as an acceptable entry for used clothing and household items. Treas. Reg. section 1.170A-13 also
    requires your records to show the method used in determining fair market value, which is why a value with its
    source, date, market and sample size behind it is more useful than a bare number.
  </p>

  <h2>Penalties for overvaluation</h2>
  <p>
    If the value claimed is 150% or more of the correct value, an accuracy-related penalty of 20% can apply to the
    resulting underpayment; at 200% or more the penalty rises to 40%. These valuation penalties apply only when the
    underpayment attributable to the misstatement exceeds $5,000. For charitable property, the reasonable-cause
    defense generally requires a qualified appraisal and a good-faith investigation of the value. A price guide is
    evidence of a good-faith investigation, not a safe harbor.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes for tax year 2026</h2>
  <p>
    The 2025 budget reconciliation act, commonly called the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (OBBBA), changed two things
    for charitable deductions beginning in tax year 2026:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Itemizers may deduct charitable contributions only to the extent they exceed 0.5% of adjusted gross income.</li>
    <li>Taxpayers who do not itemize may claim a new deduction for charitable contributions, but it applies to cash
    contributions only. Donated clothing and household items do not qualify for it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The practical effect is that noncash donations matter only to itemizers, and donors who bunch several years of
    giving into one year are more likely to cross the $5,000 similar-items threshold that requires an appraisal.
  </p>

  <h2>What this dataset is not</h2>
  <p>
    DonationValues publishes statistics over observed thrift prices. It is not an appraisal of any particular item,
    it does not know the condition of your item, and it does not replace the receipts, acknowledgments and forms
    listed above. Under IRS rules the donor determines fair market value.
  </p>

